ams and then we  divide it by the volume which is 3  centimeters cubed so as you can see the  density is going to be 6 divided by 3 is  2 and to find the unit I do grams per  centimeter cubed so that's how to find  the density of a solid let's imagine now  we want to find the density of a liquid  let's say some substance similar to  syrup so first thing I do is I get an  electronic balance and I get a measuring  cylinder because I need to find the mass  of the measuring cylinder by itself so  the mass let's say this measure cylinder  let's say is 20 grams what I then need  to do is I need to measure the mass of  the measuring cylinder which now  contains the liquid so I choose a select  volume to put into my measuring cylinder  now what I'm going to select is a  hundred centimeters cubed because that's  a sensible volume to use so I've put my  hundred centimeters cubed in there so  there's my particular liquid it's not  water it's got let's imagine this one  has a mass of a hundred and sixty grams  so that's the measuring cylinder  containing the water so what I need to  do next is I need to find the mass of  the liquid so not water of the liquid  just by itself now to do this I'm going  to take the total mass of the measuring  cylinder containing the liquid and take  away the mass the mass of just the  measuring cylinder so that's a hundred  and sixty takeaway 20 which equals a  hundred and forty grams now that I've  got this mass I can just use my density  calculation so to find the density of  this liquid all I need to do is just  like before my mass of the liquid which  is a hundred and forty grams divided by  the volume of the liquid which is a  hundred centimeters cubed so therefore  my density is 1.4 just do this divided  by this 140 divided 101.4 grams per  centimeter cubed it has a slightly  higher density than water because the  density of pure water is exactly one  gram per centimeter cubed so hopefully  now I've been through this we know how  to find exp